WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.790
Your monthly client review, client privacy, and your retention numbers.

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.490
This is the video for module 3 of Client Relationship Management.

3
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:19.230
Out-of-date records are dangerous because they look reliable. You might remind a client to reorder after she told you she closed her shop.

4
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:36.710
So keep five rules. Log orders within 24 hours. Leave no blank status or next action date. Keep notes dated and factual. Log every promise in a Promises tab the day you make it. And never market to anyone who asked you to stop.

5
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.710
A forgotten promise does more harm than not promising at all.

6
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.790
Once a month, sort your clients by value, meaning how much she spends, and engagement, meaning how active she is.

7
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.590
Protect your big, active clients with a personal thank-you and first choice of new items.

8
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:04.350
Re-engage big clients who have gone quiet. Call or send a voice note, and ask honestly if your service still fits.

9
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.460
Grow active clients who buy small, by mentioning a related service that genuinely suits them.

10
00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:19.450
And maintain small, inactive clients lightly, with an occasional update only if they agreed to receive one.

11
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:27.890
Zainab filters her tracker on the first Saturday of the month. Her average client spend is about 40,000 naira.

12
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:36.870
Hauwa and Esther spend well above that and are active. She sends them a thank-you voice note and first choice of December slots.

13
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:47.720
Blessing spent 64,000 but has not come for 4 months, against a usual 6 weeks. She gets a personal voice note before she is lost for good.

14
00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:57.690
Aisha and Mary come monthly for cornrows, and their records show they wear wigs at weekends. So Zainab mentions her wig revamp service.

15
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:07.410
She also finds a promised free scalp treatment for Esther in her Promises tab, and books it. One clear action for each client, in about 30 minutes.

16
00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:24.270
Nigeria's Data Protection Act 2023 sets principles for anyone who uses personal data. Collect details only to serve the client, use them only for that, keep them accurate and secure, and do not keep them forever.

17
00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.720
Keep your tracker shared with no one, and turn on two-step verification.

18
00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:35.630
Ask before adding someone to a broadcast. May I send you our monthly price list?

19
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.820
If anyone says stop, mark her record No marketing that same day.

20
00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:49.330
Never post a client's chat, photo, name or number without her clear permission. Blur names even when she agrees.

21
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.080
And if a client asks what you hold about her, show her, and correct or delete it as she asks.

22
00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:10.900
Saying yes to everything leads to late deliveries and broken promises. For example, Bisi cooks small chops in Ibadan. She has about 30 working hours a week, and a party order takes about 5 hours.

23
00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.430
So her maximum is 6 orders a week.

24
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:29.100
She keeps 1 slot free for repeat clients' urgent requests. In a busy week, she politely offers extra requests the following week, or recommends a caterer she trusts. She keeps her quality and her promises.

25
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:43.770
Feeling that clients are happy is not evidence. Numbers are. For example, Funmi supplies soap. She started January with 40 clients, gained 12 new ones, and ended March with 45 active.

26
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:56.030
Retention is clients at the end, minus new clients, divided by clients at the start. 45 minus 12 is 33. 33 divided by 40.

27
00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.950
That is 82.5 percent. Most of the clients she started with stayed.

28
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:09.510
Churn is the opposite: 100 minus 82.5, which is 17.5 percent.

29
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:19.400
Her repeat rate is clients with 2 or more orders, divided by all clients. 29 of 52, about 56 percent.

30
00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:27.940
Count honestly. Include clients who left unhappy. A modest true number that improves beats a perfect number nobody can check.

31
00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:44.190
To remember. Keep clean records and a promises log. Review your four groups every month. Ask before marketing and honour every opt-out. Know your capacity. And track your numbers every quarter, including referrals from your Source column.

32
00:04:45.440 --> 00:05:01.210
Now practise. Add a Promises tab and sort every client into one of the four groups. Check your privacy settings, add a marketing consent column, and work out your weekly capacity. Then set your baseline retention, churn and repeat rate.

33
00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.500
Continue with the lessons below.
